The following function has been deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0, and can be
hidden entirely by defining
.Ar OPENSSL_API_COMPAT
with a suitable version value, see
.Xr openssl_user_macros 7 :
.Ft EVP_PKEY *
.Fo EVP_PKEY_new_CMAC_key
.Fa "ENGINE *e"
.Fa "const unsigned char *priv"
.Fa "size_t len"
.Fa "const EVP_CIPHER *cipher"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Vt EVP_PKEY
is a generic structure to hold diverse types of asymmetric keys (also
known as "key pairs"), and can be used for diverse operations, like
signing, verifying signatures, key derivation, etc.
The asymmetric keys themselves are often referred to as the "internal
key", and are handled by backends, such as providers (through
.Xr EVP_KEYMGMT 3 )
or
.Vt ENGINE Ns s.
.Pp
Conceptually, an
.Vt EVP_PKEY
internal key may hold a private key, a public key, or both (a keypair),
and along with those, key parameters if the key type requires them.
The presence of these components determine what operations can be made;
for example, signing normally requires the presence of a private key,
and verifying normally requires the presence of a public key.
.Pp
.Vt EVP_PKEY
has also been used for MAC algorithm that were conceived as producing
signatures, although not being public key algorithms; "POLY1305",
"SIPHASH", "HMAC", "CMAC". This usage is considered legacy and is
discouraged in favor of the
.Xr EVP_MAC 3
API.
.Pp
The
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new
function allocates an empty
.Vt EVP_PKEY
structure which is used by OpenSSL to store public and private keys.
The reference count is set to
.Sy 1 .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_up_ref
increments the reference count of
.Fa key .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_dup
duplicates the
.Fa key .
The
.Fa key
must not be ENGINE based or a raw key, otherwise the duplication will
fail.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_free
decrements the reference count of
.Fa key
and, if the reference count is zero, frees it up.
If
.Fa key
is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key_ex
allocates a new
.Vt EVP_PKEY .
Unless an engine should be used for the key type, a provider for the key
is found using the library context
.Fa libctx
and the property query string
.Fa propq .
The
.Fa keytype
argument indicates what kind of key this is.
The value should be a string for a public key algorithm that supports
raw private keys, e.g., one of:
.Qo Li ED25519 Qc ,
.Qo Li ED448 Qc ,
.Qo Li X25519 Qc ,
.Qo Li X448 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-44 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-65 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-87 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-KEM-512 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-KEM-768 Qc ,
or
.Qo Li ML-KEM-1024 Qc .
.Fa key
points to the raw private key data for this
.Vt EVP_PKEY
which should be of length
.Fa keylen .
The length should be appropriate for the type of the key.
The public key data will be automatically derived from the given private
key data (if appropriate for the algorithm type).
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key
does the same as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key_ex
except that the default library context and default property query are
used instead.
If
.Fa e
is non-NULL then the new
.Vt EVP_PKEY
structure is associated with the engine
.Fa e .
The
.Fa type
argument indicates what kind of key this is.
The value should be a NID for a public key algorithm that supports raw
private keys, i.e.
one of
.Sy EVP_PKEY_X25519 ,
.Sy EVP_PKEY_ED25519 ,
.Sy EVP_PKEY_X448
or
.Sy EVP_PKEY_ED448 .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key_ex
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key
may also be used with most MACs implemented as public key algorithms, so
key types such as "HMAC", "POLY1305", "SIPHASH", or their NID form
.Sy EVP_PKEY_POLY1305 ,
.Sy EVP_PKEY_SIPHASH ,
.Sy EVP_PKEY_HMAC
are also accepted.
This usage is, as mentioned above, discouraged in favor of the
.Xr EVP_MAC 3
API.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_public_key_ex
works in the same way as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key_ex
except that
.Fa key
points to the raw public key data.
The
.Vt EVP_PKEY
structure will be initialised without any private key information.
Algorithm types that support raw public keys are
.Sy ED25519 ,
.Sy ED448 ,
.Sy X25519 ,
.Sy X448 ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-44 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-65 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-87 Qc ,
.Sy ML-KEM-512 ,
.Sy ML-KEM-768 ,
and
.Sy ML-KEM-1024 .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_public_key
works in the same way as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key_ex
except that
.Fa key
points to the raw public key data.
The
.Vt EVP_PKEY
structure will be initialised without any private key information.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_mac_key
works in the same way as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key .
New applications should use
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key
instead.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_raw_private_key
fills the buffer provided by
.Fa priv
with raw private key data.
The size of the
.Fa priv
buffer should be in
.Em *len
on entry to the function, and on exit
.Em *len
is updated with the number of bytes actually written.
If the buffer
.Fa priv
is NULL then
.Em *len
is populated with the number of bytes required to hold the key.
The calling application is responsible for ensuring that the buffer is
large enough to receive the private key data.
This function only works for algorithms that support raw private keys.
These include:
.Sy ED25519 ,
.Sy ED448 ,
.Sy X25519 ,
.Sy X448 ,
.Sy HMAC ,
.Sy POLY1305 ,
and
.Sy SIPHASH .
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_raw_private_key
also works with
.Qo Li ML-DSA-44 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-65 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-87 Qc ,
.Sy ML-KEM-512 ,
.Sy ML-KEM-768
and
.Sy ML-KEM-1024
keys, which don't have legacy numeric
.Em NID
assignments, but their raw form is nevertheless available.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_raw_public_key
fills the buffer provided by
.Fa pub
with raw public key data.
The size of the
.Fa pub
buffer should be in
.Em *len
on entry to the function, and on exit
.Em *len
is updated with the number of bytes actually written.
If the buffer
.Fa pub
is NULL then
.Em *len
is populated with the number of bytes required to hold the key.
The calling application is responsible for ensuring that the buffer is
large enough to receive the public key data.
This function only works for algorithms that support raw public keys.
These include:
.Sy ED25519 ,
.Sy ED448 ,
.Sy X25519 ,
and
.Sy X448
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_raw_public_key
also works with
.Qo Li ML-DSA-44 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-65 Qc ,
.Qo Li ML-DSA-87 Qc ,
.Sy ML-KEM-512 ,
.Sy ML-KEM-768
and
.Sy ML-KEM-1024
keys, which don't have legacy numeric
.Em NID
assignments, but their raw form is nevertheless available.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_CMAC_key
works in the same way as
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key
except it is only for the
.Sy EVP_PKEY_CMAC
algorithm type.
In addition to the raw private key data, it also takes a cipher
algorithm to be used during creation of a CMAC in the
.Fa cipher
argument.
The cipher should be a standard encryption-only cipher.
For example AEAD and XTS ciphers should not be used.
.Pp
Applications should use the
.Xr EVP_MAC 3
API instead and set the
.Sy OSSL_MAC_PARAM_CIPHER
parameter on the
.Sy EVP_MAC_CTX
object with the name of the cipher being used.
.Sh NOTES
The
.Vt EVP_PKEY
structure is used by various OpenSSL functions which require a general
private key without reference to any particular algorithm.
.Pp
The structure returned by
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new
is empty.
To add a private or public key to this empty structure use the
appropriate functions described in
.Xr EVP_PKEY_set1_RSA 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_set1_DSA 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_set1_DH 3
or
.Xr EVP_PKEY_set1_EC_KEY 3
for legacy key types implemented in internal OpenSSL providers.
.Pp
For fully provider-managed key types (see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ),
possibly implemented in external providers, use functions such as
.Xr EVP_PKEY_set1_encoded_public_key 3
or
.Xr EVP_PKEY_fromdata 3
to populate key data.
.Pp
Generally caution is advised for using an
.Vt EVP_PKEY
structure across different library contexts: In order for an
.Vt EVP_PKEY
to be shared by multiple library contexts the providers associated with
the library contexts must have key managers that support the key type
and implement the
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_keymgmt_import 3
and
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_keymgmt_export 3
functions.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_raw_public_key ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_CMAC_key
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_new_mac_key
return either the newly allocated
.Vt EVP_PKEY
structure or NULL if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_dup
returns the key duplicate or NULL if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_up_ref ,
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_raw_private_key
and
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get_raw_public_key
return 1 for success and 0 for failure.
